workedExamples.solution_text:
(a) $\begin{aligned}\frac{dP}{dt}&=0.003\times1000P\left(1-\frac{P}{1000}\right)\\&=3P\left(1-\frac{P}{1000}\right)\end{aligned}$
Thus $k=3$ and the carrying capacity is $C=1000$.
(b) $\begin{aligned}0.003P(1000-P)&=0\\P&=0\text{ or }1000\end{aligned}$
An extinct population stays at $0$; a population at carrying capacity stays at $1000$.
(c) The rate is a concave down quadratic in $P$, with roots $0$ and $1000$. Its maximum lies halfway between the roots, at $P=500$.
